.TH HD-SYNC-SKEL 1 "October 2026" "hd-base" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-sync-skel \- bring the files of /etc/skel into the home directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-sync-skel
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-sync-skel
compares the files in
.I /etc/skel
with those in the home directory of the user who runs it, and updates the
home directory:
.IP \(bu 2
A file of
.I /etc/skel
that is missing in the home directory is copied, with the folders it is in.
.IP \(bu 2
For a file that differs, the user chooses:
.B k
keeps the own file,
.B t
takes the new version from
.IR /etc/skel ,
and
.B m
merges the two.
A file that is not a text file cannot be merged.
.IP \(bu 2
Files in the home directory that are not in
.I /etc/skel
are left alone.
.PP
The merge runs in
.BR meld (1)
in a graphical session, recognized by
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
or
.B DISPLAY
in the environment, when meld is installed.
The new version is on the left and the own file on the right; save the
right side to keep the merge.
Otherwise the merge runs with
.BR sdiff (1),
the new version on the left:
.B l
takes the new lines,
.B r
keeps the own lines,
.B e
edits them in
.B $EDITOR
(nano when it is not set), and
.B q
quits and leaves the file as it was.
.PP
Taking or merging writes into the file, so it keeps its mode.
Run it as the user whose home directory is to be updated; with
.B sudo
it updates the home directory of root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when all files are handled, 1 on an error or when the user stops it with
Ctrl-C or Ctrl-D.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/skel
The files new users get.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-base (7),
.BR meld (1),
.BR sdiff (1)
